Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="negotiated-care"),{"creator": "Marilynn Abou Zeidan", "src": "negotiated-care.m4a", "embedUrl": "", "shareUrl": "", "approved": true, "duration": "46:21", "language": "English", "poster": "negotiated-care-cover.png", "posterAlt": "An elderly person with a cane and bag climbs cracked stairs toward a hospital beneath a stormy sky. Crossed-out insurance and NSSF symbols flank the stairs.", "coverCaption": "Even with insurance or NSSF coverage, access to healthcare becomes a long, broken climb.", "synopsis": "Negotiated Care examines how access to healthcare in Lebanon is shaped by institutional fragmentation, financial pressures, and informal networks known as wasta. Through interviews with a former Minister of Public Health, a patient, and an insurance-sector representative, Marilynn Abou Zeidan explores the distance between formal coverage and the lived experience of obtaining treatment.", "statement": "I chose this topic specifically from a curiosity to explore how far can personal connections be a deterministic factor in a sector governed by ministries, hospitals and insurance companies; so in a system where a logic exists at least on paper. After being myself a victim of personal connections, not in the medical sector specifically, yet in a system that closely parallels it. I was trying to bridge the gap between personal circumstances and actual lived experience in meritocratic systems.\nThe main issue of this gap is that it is regularly found in nearly every encounter with people talking about their treatment plans. On paper, we find many coverage systems through the Ministry of Public Health or the National Social Security Fund or private schemes, but in reality, this can never guarantee full treatment. Moreover, nearly every time, factors outside the formally recognized systems such as the ability to take advantage of personal sectarian-based connections, become decisive throughout this process. To address this issue I conducted the three interviews previously described. \nTaken together these insights generate a clear thesis: The Lebanese healthcare system in not a merely neutral system, instead, it is negotiated across many layers, political, financial and social. The political layer represents the first negotiation panel translated by a fragmented system with a scattered decision-making system. Second, the negotiation moves into the financial layer with inconsistent coverage and economic instability constantly reshaping it. Third, when this gap is enlarged between formal eligibility and reality, the negotiation resides in informal mechanisms; a type of secret gateway representing the last grain of hope for a large portion of the Lebanese population. Therefore, wasta is not just a side-effect of this issue in healthcare system, it becomes a primary decisive factor compensating for existing constraints. It functions at the cost of fairness and promotes a new type of inequality depending from which sect the negotiation happens instead of eradicating it.  \nUltimately, Negotiated care calls for a change in how the healthcare system should be understood. A holistic comprehension of the system should be a combination of formal as well as informal routes for access. Then, it would be possible to understand that expanding services or coverage is not a solution instead, a remodeling of the system itself from welfare to proper medical service would be a plausible approach.", "transcript": "PODCAST TRANSCRIPT: NEGOTIATED CARE\n\n[00:00] [music]\nMarilynn-Host: When you arrived then, what were you first asked or told? And how clear was the process of getting admitted or seen by a doctor?\nPatient: Well, I went to the emergency reception of a private hospital in Beirut. And the first person I spoke to was not a nurse, neither a doctor or a physician. It was an administrative clerk behind a glass window who asked me for my insurance card before even asking me what was wrong.\nMarilynn-Host: There, I take you into a moment that happens every single day in Lebanon. A patient arrives at a hospital. They are in pain, sometimes severe, sometimes urgent, sometimes life-threatening. They walk to reception and give their name. And then, something happens that is not written in any medical textbook. The first question is not always, \"What is your condition?\" It is often something else: \"Are you covered? Do you know someone here?\" Now pause on that question, because that question alone captures something much deeper than healthcare. It captures how access works in this country. And it is not an exaggeration to say that in Lebanon, this question can change the outcome of care. Examining patients’ experiences in the Lebanese healthcare system shows that access to medical services is frequently shaped by informal personal connections, commonly referred to as wasta. But what is important is not just that wasta exists; it is when and how it appears. Patients describe situations where admission to hospitals depends on personal contacts, waiting times are shortened through intermediaries, financial costs are reduced through negotiation rather than policy, and in some cases, treatment access is facilitated through non-official channels. In other words, access is not purely determined by medical urgency; it is also determined by relational proximity. And that creates a system where two patients with identical conditions may experience completely different pathways of care, not because their illness is different, but because their social networks are. Now, if we stop here, we might be tempted to describe this as corruption, or dysfunction, or simply system failure. But that would be too simple, because what is actually revealed is more complex and more uncomfortable. It shows that wasta is not an exception to the healthcare system; it is part of how the system operates under pressure—well, not under pressure, under normal conditions. And more importantly, it shows that patients are fully aware of this. They do not describe wasta as something hidden or invisible; they describe it as something necessary, but unfair.\n[02:54]\nThat contradiction is the starting point of this episode, because it forces us into a paradox: people recognize that the system is unjust and still rely on it to survive. So what kind of system produces that kind of behavior? To understand that, we need to step away from the idea of healthcare as a purely medical institution. In Lebanon, healthcare is not just about hospitals, doctors, or treatment protocols; it is also about financial systems, insurance structures, political influence, and social networks. It is a layered system where formal rules exist, but do not fully control outcomes. And when formal rules are weak, something else fills the gap: informal systems. In this case, wasta becomes a parallel access mechanism, not officially recognized, but widely understood and consistently used. And this is not occasional; it is patterned. It repeats across patient narratives, which means we are not dealing with isolated incidents; we are dealing with a structural feature of healthcare access. Now, here is where the Lebanese case becomes more analytically interesting. Because in many systems around the world, inequality in healthcare is usually discussed in terms of money or insurance coverage. But in Lebanon, there is a second layer of inequality that sits underneath that: the inequality of navigation. Two people may have similar financial status, similar diagnosis, similar urgency, but vastly different outcomes depending on who they know, who can intervene, who can magically enhance their coverage, and how quickly they can mobilize that intervention. So access becomes something that must be constructed, not simply received. And that construction takes time, knowledge, and networks. Which means healthcare is no longer only a biological system; it becomes a social process. And when survival depends on social process, inequality becomes embedded in the very moment of illness. Now, one of the most striking psychological implications is that patients do not simply criticize the system from the outside. They participate in it while criticizing it. They often describe wasta as unjust, frustrating, even humiliating. And yet, when faced with urgency, they still use it. This produces what you might call a dual reality of care: a moral reality where the system is unfair, and a practical reality where it is necessary. And people live in both at the same time. That tension is not a contradiction to be solved easily; it is a condition of survival. Because in a crisis-driven healthcare system, refusing informal mechanisms does not remove inequality; it often increases vulnerability. So people adapt, and adaptation becomes normalized. Over time, what begins as exception becomes expectation—not officially, but socially. And that is where systems change quietly, not through policy, but through repetition of behavior.\n[05:54]\nAnd that brings us to the central question of this episode: What does healthcare mean in a system where access is not guaranteed by policy alone, but must often be actively negotiated through networks of influence.\nTo explore this, we will move through three perspectives that sit inside the system in very different ways: First, a former Minister of Health who understands the system from the inside of policy and institutional constraints. Second, a patient who has lived the experience of navigating care through both formal and informal channels. And lastly, a representative from the insurance sector who sits at the intersection of finance, risk, and access regulation.\n\nEach will describe a different layer of the same reality. But the real insights will come from what happens when these layers collide. Because in Lebanon, healthcare is not only a system of treatment; it is a system of access negotiation. And in that negotiation, inequality becomes operational at the exact moment where people are most vulnerable. To see whether structure in Lebanon still has authority over outcome, I am joined now by former Minister of Health, Dr. Firass Abiad.\n==================================================\nINTERVIEW: DR. FIRASS ABIAD\n==================================================\nMarilynn-Host: How is healthcare access in Lebanon supposed to function in theory, from the moment a patient is eligible for care to the moment they receive treatment?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: So, the—in Lebanon, patients have—first of all, we have very good health system as a provision health system, in the sense that we have good hospitals, good doctors, good nurses. We do have some geographical inequality, but in general we have a good system. The main problem for some patients, especially after the financial crisis, has been coverage. Before the crisis, almost 30% of the Lebanese people had to cover their expenses out of pocket—or 30% of the expenses were paid out of pocket. The international standard, the acceptable international standard, is 10%. After the crisis, multiple crises, that number rose up to between—around 70%, which is considered catastrophic. And for that reason, you know, there are many people who have inadequate access to care, whether no access whatsoever or whether it is insufficient to their needs.\nMarilynn-Host: Who are the main actors responsible for regulating and coordinating healthcare delivery, and how are their roles divided in practice?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: So theoretically, this is a—but why do I say theoretically? Because if you look at the different funds, you see that they are under the jurisdiction, you know, or supervision of different ministries. So for example, the National Social Security Fund, NSSF, Daman, is under the Ministry of Labor. The Ta'awuniyat al-Muwazzafeen is under the—is at the Council of Ministers, Ri'asat al-Wuzara'. Insurance company, healthcare insurance company, are under the Ministry of Economy. Al-Jaysh is under the Ministry of Defense. Al-Amn under the Ministry of Interior. And if you look at, for example, Sanadiq al-Ta'adud, under the Ministry of Agriculture, li'anno ballashit al-ta'awuniyat. So you see, this fragmentation means that, you know, the Ministry of Public Health cannot really force, you know—so if they need to do anything, they have to go through, you know, either decrees or laws. But if only the ministry issues a decree, in itself it will be insufficient. So, but in theory, it is the Ministry of Public Health that is the one who is a guarantor—who is a regulator of, you know, healthcare delivery in Lebanon.\n\n[09:56]\nMarilynn-Host: Do you think there would be a solution to get over this fragmentation?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: Well, there was several attempts, including the creation of a council of guarantors, where they all sit together and discuss. The problem is, as I said, as long as nobody has the force to regulate, you know, as long as this is all voluntary, then this issue of fragmentation, or what we call, you know, ta'addud al-jihat al-daminah and all that—unfortunately, we haven't found a good solution to at least, if not unify into a unified payer system, like for example in France or some other countries, but at least to align everybody together under one system. In this, we are still very short of doing this.\nMarilynn-Host: In practice, why does being formally eligible for care, whether through public coverage or private insurance, not always translate into actual access to treatment?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: Because, again, we go back to, you know, in Lebanon, it's not just that you are eligible for care; somebody has to pay for that care. And here, this is where, you know, the public doesn't understand very well the relation between, you know, what they are paying and what they are getting, and how this is to be regulated, right? So, a lot of people in Lebanon think that just because they're eligible to care, somebody has to cover, whether it's the ministry, Daman, etc. This is not wrong. At the end of the day, somebody has to pay for this, okay? Now, we can organize that payment, like what we do through the National Social Security or sometimes the insurance companies, but because we don't have a unified body that at least regulates—what does regulate mean? That we tell everybody what is your right and what are your obligations. You know, okay, you want this care, for example, these services, you should be paying this amount, whether you pay it through taxation, through contribution, through you go and you buy it, it's fine. But the idea is, this is the package, this is what you need to pay. Because all of this is not organized, we don't have the right body that is telling people what is the package, what should be done, what should be—and in a lot of countries, they do this under what's called a Universal Health Coverage Law. So there is a law that actually organizes all of this. In Lebanon, we don't have a UHC law. We have been saying that we need one for a very long period, but it never happened.\n[12:36]\nWhy? Because in Lebanon, the political system still wants to treat care as something that is given as a favor. So, you know, you come, we give you hal-khidmeh, which you repay us back that favor, you know: 'Amalna lakk yaha, bi-'amrak hkeet ma'ak, tayyib, hkeet ma'al-makhbar aw kaza. So, nehna mnekhdmak bifouta 'al-mustashfa, mnekhdmak bnjiblak al-dawa, bil-moqabil, nehna tzakkarna bukra bil-intikhabat, aw ma ba'ref shu, aw kaza. So, whereas if you actually make it a system, and you make it like a obligation or right, then a lot of the political establishment cannot anymore use it the way it's being used in Lebanon. Then the political system in Lebanon is—has been created in a way by which this is a welfare state where, you know, people think, you know, there is a reason why the relation between \"I am paying\" and \"I am getting\" is not clear in Lebanon when it comes to the public sector. Why? Because if the relation is clear, then the politician cannot come and say, \"I did it for you.\" It's your right!\nMarilynn-Host: What factors most influence whether a patient receives timely admission, medication, or hospital care in Lebanon?\nDr. Firass Abiad: Okay, so there are many factors. The first one is availability. For example, supposing you have a patient with complex cancer. That service might not be present in Akkar, for example, where the patient is. So this is a problem. Is it available, or must they have to travel, and all, you know? So that's one factor. Second factor is who's going to pay for it. Okay, so if the patient has somebody—either he has the money or he has bought, for example, a very good insurance—then, of course, it is easy. But if they don't have this, and their guarantor, for example, like what happened with Daman, it is paying very small amounts for that service, which is unacceptable by a lot of the providers, then of course they will have access and they will get that care.\nMarilynn-Host: Can you describe a typical situation where a patient is technically covered, but still struggles to access care?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: Yeah. For example, if you look at, let's say, a bone marrow transplantation, a patient who needs a bone marrow transplantation, and the patient doesn't have any resources. They go to the, let's say, Ministry of Public Health. They say, \"Okay, you know, I am one of the most vulnerable. I need this, and I'm eligible.\" The ministry says, \"Okay, I only pay $10,000. But the operation itself costs $50,000.\" So the question is, who's going to pay the $40,000? And this is a very clear case where if the patient cannot get, for example, supports from an NGO, philanthropy, you know, other people to support, GoFundMe or whatever, they will not be able to get the service.\n[15:53]\nHost: Do you think until when we always have to rely on GoFundMe, NGOs, etc., instead of our country?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: This is where, you know, short of organizing the money and how it is being used... So, for example, if you look at how much taxation we pay in Lebanon, if you compare what we truly pay in taxation to what, for example, people pay in Europe, we pay around probably 5% of our income taxes to the government. In Europe, they pay 40%, 50%. So of course European countries can provide more for their patients. Now, why people in Lebanon don't pay more? Because they don't trust the government. They don't trust that the government, if they pay it more, it will do the right thing with it. So I'm not saying that the people are wrong, but unfortunately, a lot of the absence of trust, transparency, good regulation, also feeds into the fact that people don't entrust the government. The other thing is prioritisation. Okay, the Ministry of Public Health is receiving money, but are we spending it on the right medications? On the right packages? Are we prioritizing some things for some across Lebanon, or allowing the other? For example, in all health systems in the world, the most important part of a health system is primary healthcare and prevention, screening and so on. If you look at the actual—in actual terms, how much does the Ministry of Public Health spend on primary healthcare, it's almost 1% of the budget. International standards say 15% of the budget, or even more. So this is also issues related to prioritisation, and so forth.\nMarilynn-Host: At what point in the process do personal connections begin to matter, and in what ways do they shape access to care?\nDr. Firass Abiad: Personal connections matter even before the financial crisis, and they have always mattered. Why? Because, as I said, the political system in Lebanon is built on the idea of welfare. And in welfare, you bargain. \"I will give you something; you will give me something.\" Political allegiance, political support, and so forth. And that's why, you know, all the ministries that can be like Sihha, they were known as wizarat khadamat. It's not khadamat just in the idea of service; khadamat also in the idea of like, like favor: khadamat ya'ni two things, mazbout walla la? Yes.\n[18:40]\nSo I think that there has always been the idea, because the problem in a system—if you have a system, then it's very difficult to say, \"Ana khadamtak.\" The system actually is doing it for you. So that's why, you know, I've always thought that in Lebanon, the fact that we don't have systems was done on purpose. Because it allows this fund—this allows then people to, you know, channel connections and so forth. And by the way, sometimes it's not only the politicians, even the people... They don't mind, in what sense? Because they say, \"Okay, ana 'andi ma'arif, so I can get this more than jari or jarti or whatever.\" So even the Lebanese thinks that this is a good system because there isn't a lot—ma'arfo bikhallou to be first in line, let's say. So there is this idea that both are benefiting from the system. The one—the beneficiary is thinking, \"Ana hayda al-system li'anno ma'arfi fi mneh\" that, then I am much better than jari yalli ma bya'ref.\nMarilynn-Hostst: Would you describe these informal mechanisms as exceptions, or have they become embedded in how the system works?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: No, they are actually—the exception was to try and put systems. That's why, for example, when I was at the ministry, we digitized a lot of these mechanisms to remove the exceptions. Because the computer ma 'ando wasta. So when we digitized how we deliver medications, we digitized, for example, eligibility, we did a lot of these things. We digitized in a way so that people don't go to—you don't need to know anybody. And the exceptions, we stopped exceptions, and therefore we told people, you don't need to talk to me. If what you want is within the rules and regulations, then you're going to get it. You don't need to talk to anyone; the system will give it to you. If what you want is not included in the rules and regulations, then there is no point in talking to anyone, because we will not give it to you; you'll never get an exception. Even at one point my father needed an exception, and I didn't give anybody an exception, including my father. And that helped, at least when I was there, to put limitations to this exception acts. But again, I was fighting the system, rather than the exceptions were exceptions; they were embedded in the rules.\nMarilynn-Host: What conditions within the healthcare system make reliance on personal connections more likely?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: The absence of accountability and transparency. Because first of all, transparency means everybody can see what you're doing, okay? So that's why people, like, systems or politicians want no transparency, because then they can hide they're doing something. The next step is if they're doing something wrong, will they be held accountable, or nobody will touch them, are they untouchable? So if there's transparency and accountability, which means that somebody discovers what they're doing, they will be held accountable and they will be forced to resign, or leave, or be shamed, or all, then they will not do their acts. But if they feel that nobody will know, or even if somebody knows, they can get away with it, they will do it.\n[22:34]\nMarilynn-Host: What are the broader consequences of this reliance for fairness, trust in institutions, and inequality in access to care?\nDr. Firass Abiaad: So, if there is no trust, and there's no fairness, then really, you know, the access to care will be only for the favored few, or the lucky few, if you want. And what that means is that it will be further erosion in the trust in the system, it will be further erosion in the participation in the system, good governance. There will be a lot of social unrest, social unhappiness, and it will increase the level of, you know, instability in society, in the community. So effectively, if you want to create a society that is happy, that is assured, that trusts its government, that is willing to participate voluntarily, pay taxes, you need to create a sense of fairness. Otherwise, everybody will try to gain the system, and that will lead ultimately to dysfunction in the failed state, which is the definition of what we have in Lebanon.\nMarilynn-Host: So what you are describing is not just a policy failure; it is an institutional equilibrium under constraint, where formal systems and informal systems coexist, not because one replaces the other, but because neither is fully sufficient alone. And in that equilibrium, access becomes negotiated rather than guaranteed. Unlike more institutionalized systems, such as the United States, where healthcare access is fragmented through insurance schemes, but still supported by deep financial infrastructure and regulatory enforcement, Lebanon's system operates without a stable safety net. Here, fragmentation is not simply a feature of complexity, but a condition of survival. Hospitals, providers, and patients navigate a landscape shaped by political patronage, economic collapse, and institutional erosion. This means that access to care is not only unequal, but constantly contingent on informal negotiations, personal networks, and financial capacity. What emerges is not just a strained healthcare system, but a fundamentally reconfigured one, where crisis is no longer an exception, but the operating condition itself.\n[25:10]\nIn the next segment, we move away from policy language entirely, because what we need to understand now is not how the system is designed, but how it is experienced by the people who do not sit in ministries, but in waiting rooms, emergency corridors, and decisions made under urgency. Because whatever we can say about healthcare systems at the level of ministries or financing models, it eventually collapses into something much simpler and much more difficult to analyze: what people actually experience when they are sick. Because systems do not fail in abstraction; they fail in waiting rooms, they fail in admission corridors, they fail in moments where time matters more than explanation. The following conversation is with a patient who has agreed to share their experience navigating the Lebanese healthcare system. The focus here is not only what happens medically, but how access unfolded step by step, how decisions are made, how delays occurred, and how formal procedures interacted with informal networks, such as personal connections or wasta. With that in mind, we begin.\n==================================================\nINTERVIEW: PATIENT NARRATIVE\n==================================================\nMarilynn-Host: Can you describe the moment you first realized you needed medical care, and what you did immediately after?\nPatient: Hello. Well, actually, it was a Thursday evening, and the pain had been really building up for two days. But I kept minimizing it, saying, \"I’ll hold, I’ll hold.\" But then it became really impossible to ignore. So my first instinct was not to search for the nearest hospital or call my doctor. Honestly, it was to call my brother and ask him which hospital he thought was safe to go to. I wanted to be precise about the word \"safe.\" I did not mean medically safe, because, of course, I would be in the hands of doctors and nurses. I meant institutionally safe. You know, a place where I would not be left waiting, where my insurance would be actually honored, and where someone would take my situation seriously. Before anything could proceed, the hospital needed to verify my coverage with the insurance company. And that, of course, depending on their response, so I may be asked to provide a financial deposit. So, I was not told how much the deposit might be, and I was not told how long the verification process would take, and I was not even told what would happen if the verification came back with a partial approval rather than a full one. Well, nothing about it felt straightforward. I sat in that waiting area for over an hour without even anyone telling me what was happening or going on. I did not know if my insurance was being verified, or if it had been rejected, or if I had simply been deprioritized. The uncertainty I was experiencing was not about my medical condition. It was about whether I was going to be permitted to receive my medical care, especially that I was really in hard ache.\n[28:43]\nWhat they did not tell me was that this process routinely takes several hours, and outside standard business hours, the response time may extend considerably further, so I would have to just sit and wait. And the second layer was something internal entering to the hospital. So once the insurance response returned with a partial approval, there was a conversation going on between, as I remember, the hospital administration and a person who—I don't know who it was. They were discussing what the approval actually covered of my insurance, and I was not even part of that conversation.\nMarilynn-Host: Did your insurance coverage apply fully to your treatment—the full treatment experience—or did you encounter differences between what your policy covered and what was actually required for you?\nPatient: Well, my policy did not apply fully, to be very honest. It covered a specific ceiling for the procedure I required. The actual cost of that procedure had been repriced in a way that reflected dollar-denominated expenses rather than lira-based schedule. So the difference between what my insurer paid and what the hospital charged amount to approximately something like 35% of the total invoice. And that 35% was required from me in cash and in US dollars on the day of my discharge. I had not anticipated that, and I had not even budgeted for it. And I had entered that hospital with the assumption that holding an insurance policy meant being covered. I left having learned that coverage and protection, honestly, are not synonymous.\nMarilynn-Host: From your experience, can you describe a moment where you felt that access to care depended on something other than medical urgency?\nPatient: There was a precise moment. I was told the operating room schedule was full and that my procedure would be moved for the following morning. Approximately around two hours later, after my brother made a phone call to someone connected to the hospital administration, I was informed that there's a space had been made available that evening to move to. My medical condition had not changed in those two hours. No new clinical assessment was made or conducted. No additional information had been submitted to the insurer. The only variable that really changed was the existence of a personal connection, and the fact that it had been activated. The transition from formal to informal was not a conscious crossing of a line; it felt like the obvious next step.\n[32:01]\nMarilynn-Host: Looking back at the full experience, what does it mean to you that access to care may be shaped not only by medical need or insurance entitlement, but by who you know?\nPatient: So the formal system says: hold a valid policy, present a medical need, and receive authorized care. But the actual system says: hold a valid policy, present a medical need, and then activate whatever relational capital you have to access to, because the formal channel alone was not moving fast enough or reliably enough to be trusted. So what unsettles me is not that I used a personal connection. In that moment, with my health uncertain and really aching, I would make the same choice again without hesitation. What unsettles me is that the system has made the choice rational. It has made informal navigation not a moral failing, but a survival strategy. And a system that makes informal navigation rational has, by definition, failed as a formal institution.\nMarilynn-Host: What emerges from this account is not simply a story of individual struggle; it is a structured pattern of navigation. Across the interview, healthcare access is not described as a single institutional decision point. It is described as a sequence of thresholds: entry, assessment, waiting, financial clearance, and treatment initiation. At each of these thresholds, the patient experience suggests that formal rules exist, but do not fully determine outcomes. Instead, what determines speed, ease, and sometimes even feasibility of access is the combination of institutional availability, financial capacity, and relational proximity. There is a tension between recognition that the system is unfair and the necessity of operating within it. What we now need to understand is whether this pattern is consistent across other dimensions of the system. Not only at the level of patient experience, but also at the level of financial governance, where insurance structures, hospital billing systems, and reimbursement mechanisms may reinforce or mitigate these same inequalities. That is where we turn next. Up until this point, the conversation has moved between policy and lived experience, between how the system is designed and how it is endured. But there is a third layer that quietly governs both: the financial architecture through which care is actually made possible or denied. Because in Lebanon, access to healthcare is not only a question of infrastructure or urgency, but of who pays, when they pay, and under what conditions that payment is recognized.\n[35:01]\nInsurance, in theory, exists to stabilize this uncertainty. It is meant to translate medical need into financial coverage, to act as a buffer between patients and the cost of care. Yet within the Lebanese context, marked by economic collapse, currency devaluation, and institutional fragmentation, this role becomes far more complex. Coverage exists, but its value fluctuates. Approval exists, but it is often delayed. Reimbursement exists, but it does not always align with real-time costs. What begins to emerge is a system where insurance does not guarantee access, but rather mediates it through layers of financial verification, negotiation, and constraint. Hospitals adjust their expectations, patients anticipate gaps, and insurers operate under pressure to maintain solvency in a system where pricing itself is unstable. To understand this layer more clearly, I spoke with a representative from the insurance sector, Madame Joyce Abou Zeidan. The aim here is not simply to understand how insurance works procedurally, but to uncover how financial logic shapes the boundaries of care, how decisions about reimbursement, approval, and coverage translate into real differences in access.\n\n\n==================================================\nINTERVIEW: JOYCE ABOU ZEIDAN\n==================================================\nMarilynn-Host: From the moment a patient seeks care, what is the intended process between the hospital and the insurance company until treatment is approved and reimbursed?\nInsurance representative-Joyce Abou Zeidan: The hospital submits a prior authorization request to the insurer, accompanied by the physician's clinical assessment. The insurer reviews the request and issues written authorization, specifying the approved amount and covered ceiling. The patient receives treatment. Upon discharge, the hospital submits a detailed invoice, and the insurer settles payment within 30 to 90 days. This process is logical and protective on paper. The problem is that every stage contains a structural vulnerability. Prior authorization depends on response times that are not legally mandated. Invoice settlement depends on payment cycles not enforced with penalties. The patient at every stage holds a contractual entitlement, but possesses no enforceable right to a timely response, no guaranteed appeals process, and no institutional recourse when the insurer delays or denies.\nMarilynn-Host: Where within this process are delays or complications most likely to occur under normal conditions?\nInsurance representative-Joyce Abou Zeidan: Delays cluster at two structurally predictable points. The first is prior authorization. Insurance companies in Lebanon do not operate 24-hour authorization departments. Outside business hours, the process slows or stops entirely. For emergency cases, this creates triangular paralysis: the hospital cannot proceed without written authorization; the patient cannot receive care without the hospital proceeding; and the insurer is unreachable. That's a design flaw, not a crisis failure. The second is invoice settlement. Disputes over covered and excluded line items extend payment cycles from 30 to 90 days to 6, 9, or 12 months. That liquidity problem compounds over time and directly affects hospital willingness to accept insured patients without upfront guarantees.\n[38:39]\nMarilynn-Host: In practice today, why does being insured not always translate into immediate or guaranteed access to care?\nInsurance representative-Joyce Abou Zeidan: Because insurance in Lebanon has always been a conditional promise, and the conditions have become impossible to meet. What collapsed was not primarily the financial architecture of insurance. What collapsed was the institutional trust that makes any insurance system function. Insurance is a social contract. It works only when all three parties believe the contract will be honored. In Lebanon, trust has eroded in all three relationships simultaneously: The insured does not trust their policy will be honored at face value. The hospital does not trust the insurer will pay agreed values. The insurer does not trust the hospital's invoice to reflect legitimate costs. When all three parties lose faith, the formal system becomes a procedural shell, and access becomes a function of who can compensate for what that framework fails through other means. My clients have policies. Their policies say they are covered. They arrive at the hospital and discover that coverage is a legal category, and access is a practical one. And that the distance between those two things is measured in cash deposits, authorization delays, and phone calls to people they know.\nMarilynn-Host: Have you observed changes in how hospitals deal with insured patients, particularly regarding upfront payments or prioritization?\nInsurance representative-Joyce Abou Zeidan: Today, financial eligibility is established before medical assessment. Upfront dollar deposits are required before admission, regardless of policy validity, calculated to cover the anticipated gap between reimbursement ceiling and actual billing—a gap the patient is not informed of in advance.\nMarilynn-Host: Can you describe a typical situation where a patient is technically covered by insurance, but still faces barriers to receiving timely treatment?\nInsurance representative-Joyce Abou Zeidan: A client with a valid comprehensive policy presented at a private Beirut hospital with chest pain. The insurer's authorization line was unstaffed. The hospital refused to proceed without written authorization. The family was asked for a dollar deposit equivalent to three months of insurance premium to expedite admission. The deposit was made; authorization arrived five hours later. At discharge, the insurer covered 62% of the invoice. The remaining 38% was billed directly to the patient. Six months of contesting produced no resolution. He was covered. He paid a deposit to be admitted. He paid 38% out of pocket at discharge. That's not an extreme case; that is a representative case. The formal system functioned exactly as designed; the design is the problem.\n[41:50]\nMarilynn-Host: From your perspective, are these informal mechanisms exceptions, or have they become part of how the system operates in practice?\nInsurance representative-Joyce Abou Zeidan: They are not exceptions; they are the system. The formal system processes the paperwork; the informal system moves the patient. The clearest evidence is behavioral. Patients activate informal networks in parallel with formal processes from the first moment, because experience has taught them that the formal process alone is insufficient.\nMarilynn-Host: What are the consequences of this for fairness between patients, and for trust in both insurance companies and healthcare institutions?\nInsurance representative-Joyce Abou Zeidan: The deepest consequence is that the system has made informal navigation not a moral failing, but a survival strategy. The ethical weight falls not on patients who navigate informally. It falls on the institutions that made informal navigation necessary. No financial reform of the insurance system alone will restore equity or trust. Any reform that does not simultaneously address the accountability deficit, the transparency deficit, and the institutional trust deficit will produce a more efficient shell around the same dysfunctional core.\nMarilynn-Host: What becomes evident through this conversation is that insurance, rather than functioning as a stable guarantor of access, operates as a financial filtering mechanism within a structurally unstable system. The representative's responses reveal that many of the barriers patients experience—delays, partial coverage, upfront payments—are not incidental failures, but direct consequences of how financial risk is managed under crisis conditions. The issue of reimbursement emerges as particularly central. As described, reimbursement is no longer a straightforward administrative process, but a prolonged and uncertain negotiation shaped by currency instability, pricing volatility, and limited regulatory enforcement. This dynamic reflects what has been described in the literature as a system shaped by distributed responsibility without centralized accountability, where no single actor fully guarantees access. Insurance does not fail to guarantee access because it is poorly designed in isolation, but because it operates within a broader environment where pricing, regulation, and governance are unstable. In this sense, insurance becomes a lens through which the deeper logic of the system is revealed. It shows that access to healthcare is not only determined by need or entitlement, but by the ability of institutions to absorb financial risk, and by the capacity of individuals to navigate the gaps that emerge when they cannot.\n[44:41]\nWhat emerges across these three perspectives is not a fragmented set of stories; it is a single system seen from different structural positions. And what becomes clear is that healthcare in Lebanon cannot be reduced to one explanatory layer. It is not only a policy system, it is not only a financial system, and, of course, it is not only a lived-experience system. It is all three operating simultaneously through formal mechanisms, institutional gaps, and informal relational structures that bind them together in practice. So the central question is no longer only who receives care, but rather, which kind of system determines how care becomes reachable in the first place? And in Lebanon, the answer is not singular. It is a system where access is continuously constructed through institutions, finances, and relationships operating simultaneously. A system where formal universality exists in principle, but conditional accessibility exists in practice, and where survival often depends not only on need, but on navigation. What remains unresolved is not whether the system is functional or dysfunctional; it is the fact that it is both at the same time: functional in producing the best care, dysfunctional in distributing access. And that tension between operation and inequality is where the reality of healthcare in Lebanon ultimately sits. Then maybe the question was never just what they ask at the door of the hospital. Maybe it's why that question decides everything that follows, and how long we're willing to accept a system where the answer matters more than the illness.\n\n[46:21] [heartbeat fades out]", "credits": "Primary sources:\nFormer Minister of Public Health- Dr. Firass Abiad Beirut, 2026\nPatient, Beirut, 2026\nInsurance sector representative- Joyce Abou Zeidan, Beirut, 2026\n\nSecondary sources:\nResearch for Health in Conflict (RHC) “How politics made a nation sick. The political economy of health in Lebanon.” Conflict & Health Research Group under R4HC-MENA programme, 2022. https://r4hc-mena.org/wp1/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/The-political-economy-of-health-in-Lebanon.pdf\nAoun, N. & Tajvar, M. “Healthcare delivery in Lebanon: a critical scoping review of strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats”. BMC health services research, 2024. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-024-11593-w \nBou Sanayeh, E., & El Chamieh, C. “The fragile healthcare system in Lebanon: sounding the alarm about its possible collapse”. Health Economics Review, 13(1), Article 21, 2023. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13561-023-00435-w \nJawad, A. S., Chamoun, N., & Merashli, M.“Disastrous decline of the healthcare system in Lebanon. Journal of the Royal Society of Medicine, 116(1), 27–30, 2023. https://doi.org/10.1177/01410768221144760 \nReferences 1-4 explain the nature of healthcare system in Lebanon affected by political affiliations, and the fragmented decision-making system forming a fragile policy.\nGhannam, D., Angelé – Halgand, N., & Kosremelli-Asmar, M. “Fragmentation of healthcare systems: challenges through patients’ eyes”. International Journal of Qualitative Studies on Health and Well-Being, 20(1), 2598719, 2025. https://doi.org/10.1080/17482631.2025.2598719 \nKhalife, Jade, Björn Ekman, Walid Ammar, Fadi El-Jardali, Abeer Al Halabi, Elise Barakat, and Maria Emmelin. “Exploring Patient Perspectives: A Qualitative Inquiry into Healthcare Perceptions, Experiences and Satisfaction in Lebanon.” PloS One 18 (8): e0280665, 2023. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0280665 .\nReferences 5 and 6 focus on the translation of the systemic fragmentation into the real lived patients’ cases, where a new form of inequality emerges in light of constraints imposed by a corrupted system through informal networks to guarantee access to healthcare.\nYamout, R., Khalil, J., Raven, J., Fouad, F. M., & Mansour, W. “Navigating turbulence: analyzing the resilience of Lebanon’s healthcare system in a multi-crisis scenario”. Health Research Policy and Systems, 23(1), Article 120, 2025. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12961-025-01382-0 \n“United States: Health System Review 2020 by the European Observatory.” OBS. 2020. https://eurohealthobservatory.who.int/publications/i/united-states-health-system-review-2020? \nReferences 7 and 8 show that Lebanon has always been within a crisis-dependent welfare system, that can be contrasted with the US that easily overcomes fragmentation and operates in a strict policy-based framework. \n"});
